from mk import mk
mk("XnSE9X0-S9M","Opsix Effects Mode: The Secret to New Sounds",
 "fm-operator-inline-effects-delayed-modulator-bell-noise-comb-string",
 "Effects inside an FM operator chain: per-operator EQ/phase/delay/comb/drive/decimator/waveshaper/punch applied to the signal before it modulates or is heard, for delayed-modulator bells, noise-excited comb strings and punchy FM attacks",
 "Treat each FM operator slot as an optional in-chain processor rather than a master effect, so the processed waveform goes on to modulate the next operator or is heard. Recipes: (1) a peak-EQ or phase (all-pass-like, with feedback) stage on a saw operator, with its frequency moved by mod wheel or LFO, for oscillator-level phasing; (2) a short delay with feedback on the modulator, which gives bells an inner 'chamber' echo when its ratio rises and its time drops, with a short envelope; (3) a comb resonator fed by a very short noise-burst operator, making a physical-model string, copied into three pairs (detuned, an octave up) and finished with chorus, delay and reverb as a 'virtual guitar'; (4) drive, decimator (bit/sample-rate reduction for early 8-bit wavetable tone), waveshaper and 'punch' on a modulator for aggressive FM attacks. Delta vs our KS-pluck, tuned-delay-resonator and FM ratio skills: placing these processors inside the modulation path.",
 ["FM synth whose operators can be processors (or a Web Audio graph where a modulator passes through Biquad/Delay/WaveShaper nodes before connecting to the carrier's frequency AudioParam, implementation note)","Mod wheel / LFO routing","Chorus, delay and reverb for finishing"],
 [("notes","midi","Bell, string or lead notes",True),("noise_source","audio","White-noise operator for comb excitation",False)],
 [("fm_fx_patch","audio","Bell with inner echo, comb-string 'virtual guitar', phased saw, lo-fi or punchy FM voice")],
 ["Effects in this mode act on the operator signal in the chain, not on the final audio output like a master reverb.","Changing the noise operator's ratio does nothing; tune the comb pairs via pitch/detune instead.","The transcript gives no numeric settings beyond 4-bit decimation; set amounts by ear.","Web Audio note: a modulator delay inside an FM path needs a DelayNode between the modulator and the carrier's frequency param; very short feedback delays need an AudioWorklet (implementation note)."],
 [("Single-operator EQ/phase: put a saw on an operator, switch it to an effect slot with peak EQ (centre frequency + gain) or a phase stage; raise the phase feedback, then map mod wheel or a slow LFO to the effect frequency and/or mix. Mix 0 = only the incoming operator; 100% = only this operator.","phase/eq","Oscillator-level phasing/EQ sweep, no master phaser used"),
  ("Delayed-modulator bell: plain FM pair. Set the modulator (operator 2) to a short delay effect and raise its feedback; you hear the delay inside the modulation. Raise the modulator ratio (more harmonics), then shorten the delay time, give it a short envelope and increase the carrier's release.","delay-mod","Bell tone with an echo 'inside the bell chamber'"),
  ("Comb string: carrier operator set to a comb (resonator) effect fed by operator 2. With a sine source it just sounds like overdrive. Switch the source to noise with a very short, clicky envelope, set the mix to only the effect, raise comb feedback for guitar-like tones, lengthen the source envelope for longer strings, and raise the carrier's release.","comb","Physical-model plucked string from noise + comb"),
  ("Virtual guitar: copy the comb pair to two more operator pairs (three identical sets). Detune one pair via pitch, and set the third an octave higher with a bit of detune, then combine all three and add chorus, delay and reverb.","stack","Wide, chorused multi-string comb instrument"),
  ("Drive and distortion on an operator: distortion pushes a sine toward square-like harmonics (mix it back in); 'drive' on a saw acts like an analog oscillator overdriving a VCA/filter, adding subtle warmth.","drive","Warmer or harder operator tone"),
  ("Decimator: reduce bit depth (e.g. 4-bit) and sample frequency on a saw to recreate early 8-bit digital/wavetable-era oscillators. Lower frequency = lower resolution.","decimator","Stepped, 'computer-gamey' overtone-rich saw"),
  ("Waveshaper on a sine or saw operator: choose shaping curves to make wavetable-like waveforms (e.g. a 'super square'), and stack it in the modulation chain as a base layer.","waveshaper","New static waveforms from simple sources"),
  ("Punch on the modulator: set operator 2 to the punch effect and raise it so the FM attack hits harder; useful for aggressive attacking sounds.","punch","Exaggerated FM transient")],
 ["Raising phase feedback makes the oscillator-level phasing clearly audible on an analyser/by ear.","The delayed-modulator bell shows an audible echo inside the tone that changes with delay time.","Noise burst → comb with high feedback yields a pitched string; a sine source does not.","Decimator at 4-bit shows visible stair-steps on a scope."],
 ["fm","operator","comb","physical-model","bell","delay","decimator","waveshaper","web-audio"],
 note="Hardware FM synth walkthrough; mapped to generic operator-in-chain processing. Like/subscribe outro dropped.")

mk("X4TPAWQw4ss","I Tried Building an Analog Synth Inside the Opsix…",
 "operator-osc-filter-pairs-three-filters-into-master-fm-into-filter",
 "Build subtractive 'architectures' from operator pairs: six oscillators with their own envelopes, or three oscillator→own-filter blocks (LP / LP+sub / fixed-frequency HP) into a master bandpass, FM pairs into filters, and series HP→LP",
 "Use a 6-operator engine (or any modular graph) as a configurable subtractive synth in two ways. (A) Six parallel carriers into one master filter, each with its own envelope (VCA before the filter). Layers: two HD saws an octave apart with detune and staggered attacks, a square with LFO-driven PWM, a bright saw with a sharp attack for transient bite, a triangle with width and self-feedback toward saw, and noise whose level is moved by an LFO. Then cutoff, envelope amount, resonance, slight LFO, key tracking, exciter, auto-pan and spring reverb. (B) Turn carriers into filters fed by their modulator to get three independent oscillator→filter blocks: MS-20-style LP with velocity on the envelope; LP with the filter's own sine underneath as a sub; HP switched from key-ratio to fixed frequency for strings, with LFO-driven PWM on the source. All three feed a master bandpass with its own envelope. Then feed FM pairs into the filters for glassy tops, and model classic voice layouts (two oscs in series through HP then LP). The delta vs our multi-envelope level-morph pad is per-oscillator filters, fixed vs key-ratio filter frequency, and FM-into-filter.",
 ["Engine with per-voice oscillators that each have an envelope, and assignable filters (Web Audio: per-oscillator GainNode envelopes and BiquadFilterNodes; fixed vs key-tracked frequency set in code, implementation note)","LFOs routable to pulse width, level and cutoff","Exciter/auto-pan/spring-style reverb (optional)"],
 [("notes","midi","Pad, string or lead chords",True),("architecture","string","A: six oscillators → master filter; B: three osc→filter blocks → master filter",True)],
 [("subtractive_patch","audio","Layered six-oscillator subtractive patch or multi-filter string/pad voice")],
 ["Putting the VCA (envelope) per oscillator before the shared filter is reversed from classic analog order, which is what allows independent envelopes per oscillator.","A filter on key-ratio mode key-tracks its cutoff; switch to fixed frequency to set an absolute cutoff.","Release tails must be lengthened on the oscillator (source) envelope as well as the filter's.","Values are by ear; the transcript names no numeric settings beyond the octave offsets."],
 [("Architecture A: choose an algorithm where all six operators are carriers (none modulating each other). Osc 1 = saw (HD/brighter version), osc 2 = saw an octave lower with some detune. Give both full sustain; soften the lower one's attack so it enters more slowly, and add release.","layer 1-2","Two independent saws whose envelopes are not tied together"),
  ("Osc 3 = square with an LFO on pulse width (use a spare LFO, set to taste). Osc 4 = bright, higher saw with a sharp attack envelope for bite. Osc 5 = triangle with width adjusted and some self-feedback (pushing it toward saw), used as the lower layer.","layer 3-5","PWM movement, attack transient and extra thickness"),
  ("Osc 6 = noise with a slow envelope, and an LFO on its level so it comes in and out.","layer 6","Breathy noise that swells periodically"),
  ("Master filter: lower cutoff, add filter-envelope amount and resonance, a slight LFO on cutoff and key tracking (cutoff opens up the keyboard). Add exciter, auto-pan and an old-school spring reverb.","master","Six-oscillator subtractive patch"),
  ("Architecture B: pick an algorithm with three carrier/modulator pairs. Turn each carrier into a filter (its own oscillator level down) fed by its modulator set to saw. Block 1: MS-20-style low-pass with resonance, cutoff modulated by its envelope with velocity intensity.","block 1","Saw through its own resonant LP with velocity-sensitive envelope"),
  ("Block 2: another LP fed by a saw, this time keeping the filter operator's own sine mixed underneath. Because the filter's oscillator follows the pitch section, it can act like a sub oscillator (drop its pitch); detune the source slightly.","block 2","LP voice with a built-in sub/ring layer"),
  ("Block 3: high-pass filter (own mix off) fed by a square, with the filter switched from ratio (key-tracked) to fixed frequency and set by ear for strings, and an LFO on the square's PW.","block 3","Thin, string-like HP layer"),
  ("Send all three blocks into the master filter set to band-pass with its own filter envelope.","master BP","Three oscillators through three independent filters plus a master filter"),
  ("FM into filter: change an algorithm so a modulator stacks on an operator that feeds a filter (sine modulators), giving FM spectra into the analog-style filter and then the master filter (try HP or BP on top). For classic layouts, run two oscillators in series through HP then LP (MS-20-like), or a single oscillator into one filter (Polysix-like).","fm→filter","Glassy FM tops shaped by subtractive filters")],
 ["Changing one oscillator's attack leaves the others unchanged (independent envelopes).","Switching the HP block from ratio to fixed frequency stops its cutoff tracking the keyboard.","Each block's filter can be heard soloed; the master BP shapes the sum.","FM-into-filter yields a brighter, glassier spectrum than a plain saw through the same filter."],
 ["subtractive","fm","filter","architecture","pwm","multi-filter","layering","web-audio"],
 note="Hardware FM synth walkthrough; classic-synth names kept only as architecture references (MS-20/Polysix-style), no presets copied.")
